WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.772
- [Male Narrator] We in
America are immigrants

00:00:02.772 --> 00:00:05.486
or the children of immigrants.

00:00:05.486 --> 00:00:08.630
We are one people, but a people

00:00:08.630 --> 00:00:11.547
welded from many nations and races.

00:00:15.797 --> 00:00:17.338
- My name is Michelle and my family

00:00:17.338 --> 00:00:21.176
came from Scotland to Canada in 1824,

00:00:21.176 --> 00:00:23.505
and then in the 1920s
they immigrated to the US.

00:00:23.505 --> 00:00:24.620
- My great, great grandfather

00:00:24.620 --> 00:00:26.541
came to the US from China

00:00:26.541 --> 00:00:28.081
in the late 1800s

00:00:28.081 --> 00:00:29.801
and worked on the Pacific Railroad.

00:00:29.801 --> 00:00:32.107
- ...the Ukraine and Poland and they

00:00:32.107 --> 00:00:34.016
came to the States in the 1890s.

00:00:34.016 --> 00:00:36.249
- My family's from Castellammare in Italy

00:00:36.249 --> 00:00:38.034
and came here around 1910.

00:00:38.034 --> 00:00:39.340
- [Male Narrator] Then there are

00:00:39.340 --> 00:00:41.070
families like this, who have suffered

00:00:41.070 --> 00:00:42.951
a religious persecution that could

00:00:42.951 --> 00:00:44.571
drive them from their homes.

00:00:44.571 --> 00:00:46.788
- They immigrated to America after

00:00:46.788 --> 00:00:51.113
surviving separate
concentration camps in 1946.

00:00:51.113 --> 00:00:53.869
- And my mom immigrated
from Seoul, Korea in 1968.

00:00:53.869 --> 00:00:55.287
- [Male Narrator] To the man deprived

00:00:55.287 --> 00:00:57.568
of civil rights in his native land,

00:00:57.568 --> 00:01:00.056
America offers the hope of freedom.

00:01:00.056 --> 00:01:01.910
- My parents and I traveled to

00:01:01.910 --> 00:01:03.454
the United States after the Vietnam War

00:01:03.454 --> 00:01:04.848
from a Thai refugee camp.

00:01:04.848 --> 00:01:08.265
- My mom came here from Nicaragua in 1979

00:01:09.317 --> 00:01:12.231
just before the revolution broke out.

00:01:12.231 --> 00:01:15.347
- My father's side of the
family came here by force.

00:01:15.347 --> 00:01:17.572
They are descendants of slaves

00:01:17.572 --> 00:01:19.500
and my mother's side of the family

00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:22.017
came here by choice as immigrants.

00:01:22.017 --> 00:01:25.417
- My dad is from Trinidad
and my mom is from

00:01:25.417 --> 00:01:28.166
Guyana and they moved
here in their late 20s.

00:01:28.166 --> 00:01:29.640
- [Male Narrator] And there are those

00:01:29.640 --> 00:01:30.777
who have known it firsthand, the

00:01:30.777 --> 00:01:32.913
political oppression of
autocratic governments.

00:01:32.913 --> 00:01:35.434
- I was born in Jalalabad, Afghanistan

00:01:35.434 --> 00:01:39.184
and I came to America
with my family in 1982.

00:01:40.229 --> 00:01:42.116
Russia had invaded Afghanistan

00:01:42.116 --> 00:01:44.873
at the time and millions of
Afghans fled the country.

00:01:44.873 --> 00:01:47.416
- My parents are from northern Iraq

00:01:47.416 --> 00:01:50.583
and my parents came to the US in 1984.

00:01:51.873 --> 00:01:53.201
- Everything was going well until

00:01:53.201 --> 00:01:56.790
the revolution came, then
everything turned into chaos.

00:01:56.790 --> 00:01:59.546
My family immigrated from Iran

00:01:59.546 --> 00:02:02.334
to Tampa, Florida in 1987.

00:02:02.334 --> 00:02:06.108
- Well my mom came to
America from Ireland in 1987.

00:02:06.108 --> 00:02:08.000
- My parents migrated
to Seattle, Washington

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:10.745
from León, Guanajuato about 30 years ago.

00:02:10.745 --> 00:02:12.215
- My parents immigrated to this

00:02:12.215 --> 00:02:14.882
country in 1989 from Bangladesh.

00:02:16.179 --> 00:02:18.081
- My family and I moved to America

00:02:18.081 --> 00:02:20.237
from Russia in the early 90s.

00:02:20.237 --> 00:02:22.129
- My family is from India.

00:02:22.129 --> 00:02:24.747
I came to the United States

00:02:24.747 --> 00:02:26.330
about 10 years ago.

00:02:27.596 --> 00:02:29.631
- My mom, my dad, myself, and my siblings,

00:02:29.631 --> 00:02:31.752
we all moved here about 10 years ago

00:02:31.752 --> 00:02:33.245
when I was 16 from Iran.

00:02:33.245 --> 00:02:34.516
- [Male Narrator] Millions of

00:02:34.516 --> 00:02:36.656
every nation and calling, drawn

00:02:36.656 --> 00:02:38.904
together by their vision of America,

00:02:38.904 --> 00:02:42.608
a vision of land,
opportunity, and freedom.

00:02:42.608 --> 00:02:44.779
- I don't know how my story is

00:02:44.779 --> 00:02:48.267
any different than the
Irish and the Germans,

00:02:48.267 --> 00:02:50.526
and the Polish, and the Scottish

00:02:50.526 --> 00:02:54.038
who came to America and did
absolutely amazing things.

00:02:54.038 --> 00:02:56.817
- Both sides of the family are patriots.

00:02:56.817 --> 00:02:57.997
They love this country.

00:02:57.997 --> 00:03:00.136
They worked very hard for this country.

00:03:00.136 --> 00:03:02.082
So in my opinion it doesn't really

00:03:02.082 --> 00:03:05.915
matter how they got here,
but why they stayed.

